1: # P3F.35. Every one-vertex cusp fan and the prime fixed-set obstruction
2: 
3: **Proved.** The full argument has passed independent audit.
4: 
5: For the whole-zero \(I_1,e=1\) cusp, let the allowed triangulation
6: have vertex set \(\mathbb Z^2\), period group \(\Gamma=\mathbb Z^2\),
7: and unimodular triangles. Every such triangulation is the periodic
8: triangulation
9: \[
10:  T(v,w)=\{(0,v,v+w),(0,w,v+w)\}+\mathbb Z^2                 \tag{P3F.35.1}
11: \]
12: for an integral basis \((v,w)\). It is connected to the standard
13: vertical-edge triangulation by a finite sequence of the explicitly
14: described periodic diagonal flops. Each step preserves the integral
15: homology and fundamental group of the compact threefold, and therefore
16: preserves its status as the standard smooth six-sphere.
17: 
18: For every prime \(p\), the \(\mu_p\)-fixed subset inside this cusp
19: has total \(\mathbb F_p\)-Betti number exactly two, whether or not
20: the physical delta action is semifree. Consequently every separate
21: finite or logarithmic cap in a sphere member has free delta action.
22: This last assertion supplies the cap-freeness input to P3K.15 without
23: assuming semifreeness at the chosen cusp. The present statement does
24: not yet assign a return class to every fan.
25: 
26: **Proof: the exact form of the triangulation.**
27: 
28: The quotient torus has area one. Every triangle has area one half,
29: so there are exactly two triangle orbits. Translation fixes no
30: finite edge or triangle. Counting incidences gives three edge
31: orbits. Choose one triangle \((0,a,b)\); unimodularity says
32: \(\det(a,b)=\pm1\). Its three unoriented edge classes are
33: \(a,b,b-a\), which are distinct and exhaust all edge classes.
34: After the integral linear change \((a,b)\mapsto(e_1,e_2)\),
35: the only possible third vertices of a triangle incident to
36: \([e_1,e_2]\), using those edge classes, are \(0\) and
37: \(e_1+e_2\). The opposite-side condition selects the latter.
38: Likewise, across \([0,e_1]\) it selects \(e_1-e_2\), and
39: across \([0,e_2]\) it selects \(e_2-e_1\).
40: These are exactly the adjacent triangles of the periodic unit-square
41: triangulation. Propagating across edges determines the entire plane
42: triangulation. Taking \(v=a\), \(w=b-a\) gives (P3F.35.1).
43: This is an equality of the actual periodic fans after the stated
44: integral coordinate map, not a replacement by an unmarked abstract
45: triangulation.
46: 
47: The three edge classes of \(T(v,w)\) are
48: \(\{\pm v,\pm w,\pm(v+w)\}\). In the parallelogram
49: \((0,v,w,v+w)\), replacing the diagonal \(v+w\) by
50: \(v-w\) gives \(T(v-w,w)\). Conversely replacing the
51: edge class \(v\) by \(v+2w\) gives \(T(v+w,w)\).
52: Thus adding or subtracting one column of a basis from the other
53: is exactly one periodic diagonal flip. Interchanging columns
54: does not change the triangulation. Reversing one column changes
55: the sole diagonal class \(v+w\) to \(v-w\), again one flip.
56: 
57: For completeness, an explicit terminating reduction is available.
58: Write the two first coordinates as \((x,y)\). Repeatedly replace
59: the first column by the first column minus
60: \(\lfloor x/y\rfloor\) times the second, and interchange columns.
61: Each integer multiple is executed as its finite sequence of unit
62: column moves. Euclid's algorithm reaches \((\pm1,0)\), because
63: the determinant is \(\pm1\). Reverse the first column if
64: needed. The second column is now \((0,\pm1)\); reverse it if
65: needed and subtract its appropriate multiple from the first
66: column. The result is \((e_1,e_2)\). Every intermediate basis
67: and every flip have been specified, and the standard second edge
68: is the physical vertical delta direction.
69: 
70: **Proof: the actual local flop and its topology.**
71: 
72: For one selected edge orbit, each triangle contains exactly one
73: edge from that orbit. Distinct corresponding toric curves therefore
74: share no three-cone, hence no torus fixed point and no intersection.
75: Their lattice translates are disjoint. No nonzero lattice
76: translation fixes a finite edge, so in the quotient the center is
77: one embedded rational curve. The four height-one rays of either
78: adjacent parallelogram satisfy the actual integral relation
79: \(r_0+r_2=r_1+r_3\). Their determinant-one adjacent cones
80: give the normal bundle \(\mathcal O(-1)\oplus\mathcal O(-1)\).
81: Blowing up its zero section gives exceptional divisor
82: \(\mathbb P^1\times\mathbb P^1\), with normal line
83: \(\mathcal O(-1,-1)\). The two ruling contractions are the
84: two fan subdivisions just described. All maps commute with lattice
85: translation and agree with the identity off the compact center;
86: they therefore descend to the actual compact models.
87: 
88: The integral blowup decomposition adds precisely one free summand
89: in degrees two and four. Primitivity can also be read directly:
90: the exceptional divisor has degree minus one on either contracted
91: ruling. Thus the common blowup gives
92: \[
93:  H^j(\widetilde X;\mathbb Z)
94:    =H^j(X_-;\mathbb Z)\oplus H^{j-2}(\mathbb P^1;\mathbb Z)
95:    =H^j(X_+;\mathbb Z)\oplus H^{j-2}(\mathbb P^1;\mathbb Z).
96: \]
97: There is no torsion quotient hidden in the additional summands.
98: Removing a real codimension-four center preserves fundamental
99: groups by general position; its normal sphere bundle and disk
100: bundle are simply connected, so van Kampen also proves invariance
101: under the blowup and either contraction. Starting with a simply
102: connected integral homology six-sphere therefore gives the same
103: groups after every step. The smooth sphere identification is the
104: same Smale and \(\Theta_6=0\) argument used in P3F.26.
105: 
106: **Proof: the exact prime fixed sets.**
107: 
108: Let \(\delta\in\mathbb Z^2\) be the primitive physical circle
109: cocharacter, and write \(\delta=a v+b w\). On the three open
110: edge strata the finite isotropy orders are
111: \[
112:  |\det(\delta,v)|=|b|,\qquad
113:  |\det(\delta,w)|=|a|,\qquad
114:  |\det(\delta,v+w)|=|a-b|.                              \tag{P3F.35.2}
115: \]
116: Here order zero denotes the full circle stabilizer. The formula
117: comes from the exact quotient lattice
118: \(\mathbb Z^2/\mathbb Z e\) for an edge direction \(e\):
119: the delta cocharacter has image \(\det(e,\delta)\) in that
120: rank-one lattice. Since \(\gcd(a,b)=1\), any prime divides
121: at most one of the three numbers in (P3F.35.2), including zero.
122: 
123: In a determinant-one triangle chart with coordinates
124: \((z_0,z_1,z_2)\), the circle has integral weights
125: \((c_0,c_1,c_2)\) with
126: \(c_0+c_1+c_2=0\). Their greatest common divisor is one:
127: the triangle rays are an integral basis of the three-dimensional
128: toric lattice and delta is primitive. Thus modulo a prime no
129: two weights can vanish; if two did, the third would also vanish,
130: contradicting primitivity. The \(\mu_p\)-fixed locus in this
131: chart is therefore either the isolated origin or one coordinate
132: axis. Those axes are exactly the edge strata selected by
133: (P3F.35.2). If a prime divides one edge order, the two local axes
134: glue through that edge to its whole rational curve; both triangle
135: fixed points lie on it and there are no further fixed points.
136: If it divides none, the fixed set consists of the two triangle
137: points. Open toric surface strata have no finite stabilizer,
138: because their two-dimensional torus lattice is the original
139: \(\mathbb Z^2\) and delta is primitive.
140: 
141: On every regular fibre the delta action is primitive translation,
142: so no \(\mu_p\)-fixed component can leave the central cusp.
143: Consequently the compact cusp fixed set is exactly
144: \(\mathbb P^1\) or exactly two points. Both have total
145: \(\mathbb F_p\)-Betti number two. A nonfree delta action in
146: a separate cap has a point fixed by some prime subgroup.
147: Its nonempty compact fixed subset is disjoint from the cusp
148: fixed set and contributes at least one to the total Betti number.
149: Floyd's Theorem 4.4, applied to the order-\(p\) diffeomorphism
150: of the compact six-sphere, bounds that total by
151: \(\sum_i b_i(S^6;\mathbb F_p)=2\), a contradiction.
152: This proves cap freeness for every allowed fan.
153: 
154: The primary theorem was checked in E. E. Floyd, *On periodic maps
155: and the Euler characteristics of associated spaces*, Trans. AMS
156: 72 (1952), Theorem 4.4, p.146; the coefficient convention is
157: stated on p.139. The exact elementary fan reduction is reproduced
158: in `code/part3/arbitrary_fan.py`.
